#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259622 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179775 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "36996ffc-7dbc-51f8-8efe-46ed2c897045" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether an Affine Transformation is Orientation-Preserving"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ccab3c0-0254-5090-9c86-829a851b15b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256913} {:answer/id #uuid "2c4c2cb2-f173-5288-817b-371883d27529" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257185} {:answer/id #uuid "60740839-3d41-5df6-8849-4084e44e6ec1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257636} {:answer/id #uuid "b74a87c3-7a0f-5446-af5b-58a1007116ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258388} {:answer/id #uuid "d661a75a-735f-5ece-a15b-6a8a834246d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258671}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b74a87c3-7a0f-5446-af5b-58a1007116ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258388} :answer-field/id #uuid "33b4cd81-c3a3-52e0-a0d4-355e8ea3aed5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259134}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d0a1ba57-9eac-5d49-9cdd-f7f25fec8c92" :question/math-academy-id "q-124192" :question/problem "Consider the affine transformation $T$ defined as\n\n$$\nx=2v+u+1,\\,y=u-v-2.\n$$\n\nLet $A$ be the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\n- $det(A)=-3$\n- The transformation $T$ is orientation-preserving" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that the given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2v+u+1 \\\\ u-v-2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}u+2v \\\\ u-v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, computing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}|=1\\cdot (-1)-2\\cdot 1=-3.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $det(A)<0,$ the affine transformation $T$ is not orientation-preserving.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259668 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179775 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "36996ffc-7dbc-51f8-8efe-46ed2c897045" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether an Affine Transformation is Orientation-Preserving"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3cbf8e90-0fcd-5e9d-ad8e-5d029388c5ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257331} {:answer/id #uuid "43d247fa-abed-5958-b466-10fc02c6fd8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257385} {:answer/id #uuid "4681c1f9-2a9f-5136-9cf7-ed7506a00643" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257404} {:answer/id #uuid "5d0eefab-460e-5b30-b83c-d3d6a7dee230" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257599} {:answer/id #uuid "7277e3e9-81db-502b-97dc-3210914ad7a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257794}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3cbf8e90-0fcd-5e9d-ad8e-5d029388c5ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257331} :answer-field/id #uuid "ced03044-c848-5e8d-a236-e8367ae6dd32" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259403}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d6b75fa0-1178-5417-bc31-861bbdef5229" :question/math-academy-id "q-124321" :question/problem "Consider the affine transformation $T$ defined as\n\n$$\nx=2v-u+2,\\,y=2u+v-5.\n$$\n\nLet $A$ be the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\n- $det(A)=-5$\n- The transformation $T$ is orientation-preserving" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, notice that the given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2v-u+2 \\\\ 2u+v-5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-u+2v \\\\ 2u+v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, computing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}|=(-1)\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 2=-5.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $det(A)<0,$ the affine transformation $T$ is not orientation-preserving.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259696 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179774 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1fd0b67f-681b-5b1d-8be9-2ab2d1ca5e66" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Polygon Under an Affine Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "75895875-a73e-5cbb-bf3d-c5aa2a0d474a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d1/d1e93d9ce18d7c75ab6432d53ae9ff72fd3fe2c89125a12dcd2d41a13100465d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257819} {:answer/id #uuid "7e96b286-9f53-5a16-a78e-11d14b7729bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/82/8254c51a8f1c9f0ca48f169fb48a938b07e5812b5be866d9ab25e5fbdffed368.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257914} {:answer/id #uuid "8db86e0e-3c7f-5fc7-8dd6-cb016f66e98b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/6b/6be30b91e0526e28530b4c5cfff6c0c5e7be53a77e587c5eb37a845b6cc0a189.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258034} {:answer/id #uuid "aac5b30f-88de-5a6d-a659-c1f1d57058a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a7/a7b171394aeccd9b807fc29ecf7c33f745c58bce3ed01bcf879a79d2edf97319.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258277} {:answer/id #uuid "c68d0b11-70cb-55fa-8c98-86952dd0839e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/27/27dbdbec3125bdef5109bf6aa549c9bcfe83175b42e660cc44977540de0ad112.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258523}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75895875-a73e-5cbb-bf3d-c5aa2a0d474a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d1/d1e93d9ce18d7c75ab6432d53ae9ff72fd3fe2c89125a12dcd2d41a13100465d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257819} :answer-field/id #uuid "047ad574-9503-5413-9fac-632c989f1e97" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259046}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6fdc3f93-8486-56f6-8744-50fa49ca621b" :question/math-academy-id "q-124561" :question/problem "![](images/79/79b09bcf02998adf6d7bf19e7d59cabdc563ff26266966440bbb49be53029ac3.png)\n\nConsider the triangle $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 1,\\,-\\frac{u+1}{2}\\le v\\le u+1}.\n$$\n\nThe transformation $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives the image of $Δ$ in the $xy$-plane under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nNotice that the given transformation is already in matrix form \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & ={{[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe transformation $T$ maps the boundary of $Δ$ to the boundary of the image. So, to find the image of $Δ,$ we find the images of each side.\n\n![](images/53/539c74bf4d397916fd7ac11089fa9500bd71cdf2278891136785d6e792431b05.png)\n\n             \n\nTo quickly carry out this calculation, we set up the following matrices:\n\n- The matrix $U$ containing all vertices of our triangle:\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ that has the same shape as $U$ and with the vector $b$ in each column:\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe image of $u$ under $T$ is given by $Au+b.$ So, by applying $T$ to $U,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AU+B & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertices of the image in the $xy$-plane are $(-1,-1),(1,-1),$ and $(-2,2).$\n\nDrawing this region in the $xy$-plane, we get the following:\n\n![](images/ad/adfe476bd2ca719996d612855ca5368bdea09370b7befcffb08a062db8587cbb.png)\n\n          \n\nFinally, since $det(A)=-1<0,$ this transformation is not orientation-preserving."}]}
